 ## Background
-The concept of ray-splat intersection evolved from the need to render 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) efficiently in real-time [G]. Standard 3DGS uses a rasterization-based approach that approximates the projection of Gaussians onto the image plane [G]. For wide-field-of-view cameras or spherical panoramas, this first-order affine approximation introduces significant artifacts, leading to [Spherical Panoramic Gaussian Splatting (SPaGS)](3042-spherical-panoramic-gaussian-splatting-spags.md) which uses exact ray-splat intersection with perpendicular planes to avoid covariance matrix inversion. Concurrently, [Ray-Splat Intersection](6816-ray-splat-intersection.md) introduced BVH-based traversal for more complex scenes, supporting both spherical approximations and true ellipsoid intersections. Numerical stability issues in quadratic solvers for quadric splats led to [Ray-splat Intersection with Numerical Stabilization](9972-ray-splat-intersection-with-numerical-stabilization.md), which switches to linear approximations when the quadratic coefficient is negligible. [Quadratic Gaussian Splatting](9978-quadratic-gaussian-splatting.md) further generalized this by using paraboloids and geodesic distances for surface reconstruction. Additionally, [Intersection Preserving](7916-ray-splat-gaussian-intersection.md) discards Gaussians that do not directly intersect with the ray based on depth reinitialization [S2], while [Ray Marching with RBF Intersection](7916-ray-splat-gaussian-intersection.md) intersects RBFs with slices perpendicular to the view direction for volume rendering [S1].
